<h1>Attribute Routing in ASP.NET Core MVC</h1>

<p>
    Attribute Routing is a routing technique where routes are defined
    directly on controllers and action methods using attributes.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Why Attribute Routing is Used?</h2>

<p>
    Attribute Routing provides more control and flexibility over URLs.
</p>

<p>
    It is commonly used in:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Web APIs</li>
    <li>REST APIs</li>
    <li>Enterprise applications</li>
    <li>Custom URL structures</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Difference Between Conventional and Attribute Routing</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Conventional Routing</th>
        <th>Attribute Routing</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Defined in Program.cs</td>
        <td>Defined on Controllers/Actions</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Centralized Routing</td>
        <td>Distributed Routing</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Less Flexible</td>
        <td>More Flexible</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Best for MVC Apps</td>
        <td>Best for APIs</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>Basic Attribute Routing Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[Route("products")]
public class ProductController : Controller
{
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    This creates the route:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Action-Level Routing</h2>

<p>
    Routes can also be defined directly on action methods.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[Route("products")]
public class ProductController : Controller
{
    [Route("all")]
    public IActionResult GetAll()
    {
        return Content("All Products");
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Final URL:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/all
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>How Controller and Action Routes Combine</h2>

<p>
    ASP.NET Core combines:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Controller route</li>
    <li>Action route</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Example:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[Route("students")]
public class StudentController : Controller
{
    [Route("details")]
    public IActionResult Details()
    {
        return View();
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Final route becomes:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/students/details
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>HTTP Method Attributes</h2>

<p>
    ASP.NET Core provides specialized routing attributes
    for HTTP methods.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>1. HttpGet</h3>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet]
public IActionResult Get()
{
    return Content("GET Request");
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Used to fetch data.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>2. HttpPost</h3>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Create()
{
    return Content("POST Request");
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Used to send or save data.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>3. HttpPut</h3>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpPut]
public IActionResult Update()
{
    return Content("PUT Request");
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Used to update data.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>4. HttpDelete</h3>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpDelete]
public IActionResult Delete()
{
    return Content("DELETE Request");
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Used to delete data.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Attribute Routing with Parameters</h2>

<p>
    Route parameters can also be used in attribute routing.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{id}")]
public IActionResult Details(int id)
{
    return Content($"Id: {id}");
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Example URL:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/products/5
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Route Constraints</h2>

<p>
    Attribute routing supports route constraints.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[HttpGet("{id:int}")]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Here:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>id must be an integer</li>
    <li>Invalid values will not match the route</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Token Replacement</h2>

<p>
    ASP.NET Core supports token replacement
    using special keywords.
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[Route("[controller]")]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    MVC automatically replaces:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
[controller]
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    with the controller name.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Example</h2>

<pre>
<code>
[Route("[controller]")]
public class ProductController : Controller
{
}
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Final route:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
/Product
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>How Attribute Routing Works Internally</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Incoming Request
        ↓
Routing Middleware
        ↓
Attribute Route Matching
        ↓
Controller Selection
        ↓
Action Selection
        ↓
Action Execution
        ↓
Response Returned
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Advantages of Attribute Routing</h2>

<ul>
    <li>More flexible routing</li>
    <li>Better control over URLs</li>
    <li>Ideal for REST APIs</li>
    <li>Easy to create custom routes</li>
    <li>Supports route constraints easily</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Disadvantages of Attribute Routing</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Routes become distributed across files</li>
    <li>Large applications may become harder to manage</li>
    <li>Can create route conflicts if not designed properly</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Points</h2>

<ul>
    <li>
        Attribute Routing defines routes directly on controllers and actions.
    </li>

    <li>
        It provides more flexibility compared to conventional routing.
    </li>

    <li>
        Attribute Routing is heavily used in Web APIs.
    </li>

    <li>
        HTTP method attributes like HttpGet and HttpPost
        are part of attribute routing.
    </li>

    <li>
        Route constraints help validate route values.
    </li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Final Conclusion</h2>

<p>
    Attribute Routing is a flexible routing approach in ASP.NET Core
    where routes are directly defined using attributes on controllers
    and action methods.
</p>